    "bitcoin_integration": "Mexico regulates crypto under the 2018 Law to Regulate Financial Technology Institutions ('Fintech Law' / LRITF) which defines 'virtual assets', elaborated by Banxico Circular 4/2019. Virtual assets are NOT legal tender (Article 30 LRITF explicitly states they are neither legal tender nor foreign currency, reaffirmed by Banxico/SHCP/CNBV 2021 warning). Financial entities may only engage in virtual-asset operations for internal purposes with prior Banxico authorization and are prohibited from offering exchange/custody/transfer services to customers. Non-financial individuals and companies may legally own, hold, trade and exchange crypto and operate exchanges/custodians without a specific license (subject to AML obligations; a July 16 2025 AML-law reform classifies VASPs as Designated Non-Financial Businesses with travel-rule, KYC and transaction-reporting duties). Stance is widely described as 'permissive yet conservative,' the central bank keeping a 'healthy distance' between crypto and the financial system. No CBDC launched."
